  "evidence": "TEXT_D: £2bn/year extra for education via +1p income tax; cut primary classes to max 30; double school books/equipment spend; +£540m/year for NHS and hire 10,000 nurses or 5,000 doctors; 3,000 more police; Independent Bank of England for economic stability; measures on jobs/training and tax/benefits; housing expansion and ending rough sleeping. TEXT_G: Single-issue focus on holding a referendum on Europe, framing a choice between a federal Europe and a common market; urgency and timing of the referendum; argues main parties’ euro-only referendum is insufficient.",
  "reasoning": "Voters’ top priorities in the period were public services (NHS, education), the economy, crime, and housing, which TEXT_D directly addresses with specific, costed commitments across these areas. TEXT_G centers almost exclusively on an EU sovereignty referendum, leaving most salient domestic concerns untouched, so it less reflects the electorate’s broad priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
